From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Abe Massry <amassry@linode.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 17:08:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] wasi: run each vCPU on a thread of its own (WASI_THREADS)

The multi-threaded WASI build (qemu-wasi's THREADS=1: wasm32-wasip1-threads,
with WASI_THREADS defined) runs multi-threaded TCG: one thread per vCPU,
next to the main loop's.  QEMU's memory is then imported from the host,
which shares it between the threads, and threads are created through
wasi-threads' thread-spawn import.  The single-threaded build is unchanged.

vCPU threads (accel/tcg/tcg-accel-ops-mttcg.c, tcg/wasm32.c): the host runs
each vCPU's TBs in a wasm instance and Store of the vCPU's own, which it
replaces from time to time to free the TB modules a Store accumulates.  So
a vCPU thread does not run its loop itself: it calls the host_vcpu_run
import, and the host calls wasi_vcpu_step(), one iteration of the loop, again
and again, through wasi_vcpu_enter(), which gives the vCPU's instance this
thread's stack and thread-local storage.

The TCG backend (tcg/wasm32):
- Each TB has a JIT handle per vCPU, as each vCPU's instance has a function
  table of its own; the handles a thread loaded are tracked per thread, and
  a TB buffer flush makes every thread forget them (a generation count).
- TB modules import the memory as shared; memory barriers become
  atomic.fence, which the interpreter runs too.  Its arena of decoded
  programs is shared under a lock, and its decoder state is per thread.
- TCG_TARGET_DEFAULT_MO: x86 guests need no barriers for ordinary accesses,
  as TB code keeps them in program order and x86-64 hosts keep TSO.
- A TB whose module the host still has is reloaded by every vCPU, not only
  by the one that brings its shared run count to QEMU_JIT_WARM.

Undone for WASI_THREADS: the single-threaded shortcuts (inline run_on_cpu,
vCPU stop and creation without waiting, time slices, RCU callbacks run by
the main loop), so that QEMU's own multi-threaded code runs.

The main loop sleeps with the BQL released.  It sleeps in the host, out of
reach of event notifiers (and glib-wasi never sets notify_me), so
aio_notify() from another thread wakes it through the host_main_wake
import.  Event notifiers are atomic.

The guest's timer tick: the local APIC timer fires on a grid of
QEMU_TICK_SLACK_MS (default 30 ms), which the single-threaded build gets
from its time slices.  At 1000 ticks a second, ticks took half of a busy
guest's time.

meson.build: CONFIG_ATOMIC64 for wasm32 with atomics.  The link test fails
on wasm-ld for an unrelated reason (meson's --start-group), and without it
every 8-byte guest access missing the TLB took the stop-the-world
exclusive path with more than one vCPU.

Threads get 16 MiB stacks, as a vCPU thread's stack also holds its vCPU
instance's frames.
